The fix is in place. Here's a summary of what I did.

## Problem

A view containing a `LEFT JOIN` created with `join_use_nulls = 1` stores `Nullable(...)` column types in its metadata. When that view is later queried with `join_use_nulls = 0` and the analyzer enabled, the actual execution plan produces **non-nullable** types instead.

During filter propagation into the nested view (`collectFiltersForAnalysis` in `src/Planner/Planner.cpp`), the outer `WHERE` predicate (`post_filter`) is injected as a `FilterStep` on top of the dummy plan so predicate push-down works. The existing code only verified each filter input existed in the plan header **by name** — never by type. When the propagated filter's expected column type (nullable, from view metadata) mismatched the actual header type (non-nullable), building/applying the `FilterStep` caused a `SIGSEGV`.

## Fix

In `src/Planner/Planner.cpp`, I added a type-equality check alongside the existing name check when validating `post_filter` inputs against the plan header:

```cpp
if (!input->result_type->equals(*header.getByName(input->result_name).type))
{
    all_inputs_present = false;
    break;
}
```

When any propagated filter input's type doesn't match the corresponding header column type, `all_inputs_present` becomes false, so the `FilterStep` is **skipped entirely** rather than applied with mismatched types. The query then executes without the pushed-down filter (correctness preserved — the outer query still filters normally) and returns the correct matching row instead of crashing.

This uses the same `DataTypePtr::equals` idiom already present elsewhere in the file (line 1015), so no new includes are needed. Changes are source-only, on a new branch `fix-view-filter-type-mismatch`; I did not touch anything under `tests/`.

Note: there's no build directory in the workspace, so I verified the fix by API/idiom consistency rather than a full compile (a from-scratch ClickHouse build is prohibitively long). The change matches an existing, compiled pattern in the same file.
